EXT. SALT FLATS TELEGRAPH RELAY - DAY

White to every horizon. A shack on stilts. A single pole line running away into heat shimmer. The sky is the color of a new nickel.

SADIE MULVANEY, 30s, hat low, Winchester across her lap, sits on the porch rail. She is shelling pistachios with one hand.

ELIAS CROW, 50s, relay operator, bald, sleeves rolled, sits on a barrel beside her, drinking from a tin cup. Calm. Too calm.

Inside, through the open door: the steady TAP-TAP-TAP of the key.

SADIE
Wire's working.

ELIAS
Always does.

SADIE
Thought they cut the line at Dry Fork.

ELIAS
They did.

She looks at him. He keeps drinking.

A dust plume. Three riders, far off, coming slow.

TEODORA VEGA, 20s, a bandolier and a bad limp, steps out of the shack carrying a coffeepot. She sets it down on the boards.

TEODORA
Riders.

SADIE
I see them.

TEODORA
Elias, did you --

ELIAS
Sit, Teo. Pour.

She pours. Sadie accepts a cup. The riders grow.

PIKE, 40s, a grey duster, leads them in on a bay mare. Behind him, WHISTLER, 20s, a boy with a tin whistle in his teeth, and no one else. Just two.

They stop at the hitching rail. Pike tips his hat.

PIKE
Miss Mulvaney.

SADIE
Pike.

PIKE
Heard you were dead.

SADIE
Heard the same about you.

Whistler blows a thin note and grins. Teodora's hand drifts toward her hip.

TEODORA
Tell the boy to quit that.

WHISTLER
Tell the lady I'm sorry.

PIKE
Easy, Whistle.

ELIAS
Gentlemen. There's coffee. There's shade. Nobody needs to die before noon.

PIKE
Elias, you old rat. You look well.

ELIAS
I look the same.

SADIE
(to Elias)
You knew they were coming.

ELIAS
Didn't know. Hoped.

SADIE
That's not the same thing.

TEODORA
What did you do, Elias?

PIKE
He sent the wire.

Quiet. The tapping inside goes on. No one is at the key.

Sadie looks at the door. Then at Teodora. Then at Elias, who sets his tin cup carefully on the rail.

ELIAS
Autopilot. Rig's on a timer.

TEODORA
A timer for what?

ELIAS
The Army, Teo. Forty men. Forty-one miles. They'll be here before dark.

SADIE
You sold us.

ELIAS
I sold a salt shack and a dead line. And bought a year of my life.

Whistler's whistle drops from his mouth.

WHISTLER
Pike, you said it was only --

PIKE
Shut up, Whistle.

SADIE
Teodora. Pike's going to draw. Left side.

TEODORA
I know.

PIKE
Now that's rude, Miss Mulvaney --

Sadie fires first. The Winchester cracks. Pike's hat goes spinning out over the flats.

WHISTLER
God, I'm sorry -- I'm sorry --

He drops his gun in the salt. Raises both hands. Pike sits in the dust, staring at his empty head.

PIKE
(softly)
Sadie. That was my good hat.

ELIAS
Sadie. Let them go. Both of them.

SADIE
Why should I?

ELIAS
Because the Army's still coming for us. And I'd like fewer witnesses.

She levers another round. Lowers the barrel.

SADIE
Take the horses. Ride south. Don't stop.

Pike nods. He and Whistler mount. They go, slow at first, then not.

Teodora looks at Elias. Her voice is level.

TEODORA
You get a head start. One hour. After that, I come looking.

ELIAS
Fair.

He takes his hat from the porch nail. Steps off the boards into the white glare and starts walking. No horse. No bag.

Sadie watches until he is a speck.

SADIE
Teo.

TEODORA
Yeah.

SADIE
Cut the wire.
